; Once a commodity has been written with a decimal period ($1,000.50),
; a comma that only a guess reads as a decimal comma -- a zero integer
; part, a group other than three digits, or a single comma with more
; than three digits after it -- contradicts that style.  Ledger used to
; switch $ to the decimal comma for the rest of the journal (the order
; reversed failed on $1,000.50 instead); each such amount is now an
; error naming the commodity.  The positive cases live in
; decimal-comma-order.test and decimal-comma-after-period-allowed.test,
; since an error block keeps this file out of the semantic bisimulation.

2024/01/01 Established decimal period
    A    $1,000.50
    B

2024/01/02 Zero integer part
    A    $0,075
    B

2024/01/03 Not a group of three
    A    $1,5
    B

2024/01/04 One comma, more than three digits
    A    $1,075500
    B

test bal -> 3
__ERROR__
While parsing file "$FILE", line 16:
While parsing posting:
  A    $0,075
       ^^^^^^
Error: Ambiguous decimal comma: commodity $ uses a decimal period
While parsing file "$FILE", line 20:
While parsing posting:
  A    $1,5
       ^^^^
Error: Ambiguous decimal comma: commodity $ uses a decimal period
While parsing file "$FILE", line 24:
While parsing posting:
  A    $1,075500
       ^^^^^^^^^
Error: Ambiguous decimal comma: commodity $ uses a decimal period
end test
